#: src/macros/tokens.md:6
msgid "Macros, Tokens, and Syntax"
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:8
msgid ""
"Rather than manipulating raw text, Rust macros operate on **tokens** and "
"**token trees**."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:11
msgid ""
"**Tokens** are the smallest indivisible lexical elements of the language "
"(e.g., identifiers like `foo`, operators like `+`, punctuation like `,`, and "
"literals like `123`)."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:14
msgid ""
"**Token trees** are formed by grouping streams of tokens and token groups."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:15
msgid "When a macro is called, the compiler passes it such a stream."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:16
msgid ""
"The macro processes this input stream and must return another stream of "
"tokens-and-groups that replaces the macro invocation."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:19
msgid ""
"This structure allows the compiler to validate lexical boundaries and "
"ensures that macros can never construct or return syntactically invalid, un-"
"lexable text."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:26
msgid ""
"Reiterate that because macro expansion happens _during_ AST generation, the "
"macro receives structured tokens, not characters."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:28
msgid ""
"Explain that this differs completely from preprocessors (like C/C++), which "
"perform string/text-level substitution before tokenization."
msgstr ""

#: src/macros/tokens.md:30
msgid ""
"Mention that this token-level manipulation is what makes Rust macros more "
"robust and syntax-safe."
msgstr ""
